## Abstract

This paper presents a structured comparative analysis of three engine generations used in the Hedera Hydropower digital MRV stack: **Engine V1 (baseline deterministic MRV)**, **Engine V1 Enhanced AI (multi-factor trust scoring)**, and **Engine V2 (two-tier operational verification modes)**. The analysis synthesizes architecture specifications, scoring logic, on-chain evidence artifacts, and operational policy design to evaluate methodology integrity, automation potential, regulatory acceptability, and expected verification economics. Results show that all three variants preserve ACM0002-aligned accounting logic while progressively increasing adaptive automation and evidence richness. Engine V1 provides deterministic reproducibility; Engine V1 Enhanced adds granular trust decomposition and tri-band decisioning; Engine V2 adds governance-level adaptability (strict vs evidence-rich modes), enabling context-sensitive transitions between conservative and high-throughput operations.

